Q: PHP push value into sub array of key A: <p>Getting the associative array from the function get_data_array();</p>

<pre><code>$alpha = get_data_array();
</code></pre>

<p>Assuming the below data you want to add into $alpha variable</p>

<pre><code>$key = "red";
$valOne = "blue";
$valTwo = "green";
</code></pre>

<p><strong>Method 1</strong>: If you know only few variables gonna to be added</p>

<pre><code>$alpha[$key][] = $valOne;
$alpha[$key][] = $valTwo;
</code></pre>

<p>Reason, We know the variable names which is supposed to be added in the list which is based on different variable names. PHP will create the variable if it's not exists, so when we <em>$alpha[$key][]</em> it will create a memory for the key if it's not exits, if it's already exists then append to the array at last. </p>

<p><strong>Method 2</strong>: If we are appending from another array</p>

<pre><code>$valuesToAdd = array( $valueOne, $valTwo );
</code></pre>

<p>Here, $valueToAdd can have multiple values into the array. It can be coming from some outside source, or API, or user input like tags. In that case you can do the following code</p>

<pre><code>foreach( $valuesToAdd as $element ){
    $alpha[$key][] = $element;
}
</code></pre>

<p>Here, we are looping every element from $valueToAdd and assigning to $element every time, so appending the value to the $alpha with the key.</p>

Q: how to push onto a sub-array of a multidimensional array? A: <p>Here's something I just wrote that may help.  You specify arguments in pairs:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;?php
$brands = array('Foo Global', 'Bar Global');
$titles = array('FooStar 1000', 'BarStar 1000');
$weights = array('400', '600');

function custom_array_merge() {
    $args = func_get_args();
    $arg_len = count($args);

    // Ensure there are no broken pairs. (Not a sophisticated check)
    if ($arg_len &lt; 2 || $arg_len % 2 != 0)
        return FALSE;

    $output = array();

    for ($i = 1; $i &lt;= $arg_len;) {
        $title = $args[$i-1];
        $values = $args[$i];
        foreach ($values AS $key =&gt; $value) {
            $output[$key][$title] = $value;
        }
        $i += 2;
    }

    return $output;
}

echo "&lt;pre&gt;";
print_r(custom_array_merge('brand', $brands, 'title', $titles, 'weight', $weights));
echo "&lt;/pre&gt;";
</code></pre>

<p><br>
Example output:</p>

<pre><code>Array
(
    [0] =&gt; Array
        (
            [brand] =&gt; Foo Global
            [title] =&gt; FooStar 1000
            [weight] =&gt; 400
        )

    [1] =&gt; Array
        (
            [brand] =&gt; Bar Global
            [title] =&gt; BarStar 1000
            [weight] =&gt; 600
        )

)
</code></pre>

